## Designing the canvas

The canvas shows the personally visible actors and actor views of this run. Use
`context.functions.canvas_layout_replace` from TypeScript to arrange
existing participants and explain their relationships. Free canvas is the default; do not switch
to tiles unless the user requests them or the run setup explicitly specifies them. Read its exact input and result types with
`typescript_api` before constructing a layout; they define the supported entities,
groups, shapes, connections and size limits.

For a fixed, viewport-filling workspace set mode to "tiled" and supply root. A leaf is
{entity:"@helper"} or {entity:"app:@workspace/main"}. A split has direction "horizontal"
(left/right) or "vertical" (top/bottom), two positive weights and exactly two children.
Children can be split again. For "50:50, app left, chat right", use:
{mode:"tiled",root:{direction:"horizontal",weights:[1,1],children:[
  {entity:"app:@workspace/main"},{entity:"@helper"}
]}}. Use [2,1] for two thirds and one third. Use an outer vertical split and an inner
horizontal split for one pane above two panes below. Resolve the user's actual actor and app
names before calling; the example names are not existing participants.

Tiled mode disables canvas pan, zoom and overview. Users with Actors access can remove panes
and drag actors/apps from the header or pane title onto docking targets. Split dividers remain
resizable without Actors access.
Personal user arrangements take precedence over program suggestions until the user resets them.
Do not repeatedly resend a layout to fight a personal override. Newly created participants stay
available in the header without changing existing tiles. A mode/root-only call preserves the free
canvas arrangement; {mode:"free"} restores it. A root-only call implies tiled mode; root:null
clears the tiles. Free-layout updates supply nodes and replace the explicit nodes, shapes and
lines together, so include everything that should remain there.

Activate actor programs before placing their views. Reference a view as `app:@handle/view-key`
or `app:program-name/view-key`, using the handle, program name and view key you chose. The server
resolves the actual view ID and rejects unknown views before storing the layout. Do not invent
or copy generated view IDs. The same references work in nodes and connection endpoints.
`actor_view_set_visibility` uses the same package/view or @handle/view names without the `app:` prefix.

Place the essentials first and update the layout when the participants or flow change. The host
adds unplaced visible participants and visible actor views automatically in free mode. Actors with their own
views start hidden; their views remain on the canvas and the Actors list opens their inspector.
A layout never overrides the user's personal actor visibility or connection choice. The user
participates through the chat and does not need a separate canvas node.

Choose arrangements by meaning: peers in a discussion, a sequence of work stages, a hierarchy or
a collection of independent items. Keep groups small enough to read, preserve a useful order and
add a frame only where a visible boundary helps. Leave space for expanded content and connection
labels instead of packing cards tightly together.

Connections should explain actual relationships. Give them short, meaningful labels and choose
direction and line style deliberately. The canvas does not infer the connections for you. Every
connection must join existing entities. Shapes can represent stages, decisions or topics that are
not actors; use them to explain the work without inventing additional participants.
